1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,105
Good evening from the OROS closing desk.

2
00:00:03,105 --> 00:00:09,005
This morning the system named no pick, so tonight there is nothing to grade.

3
00:00:09,005 --> 00:00:13,740
No setup, no trade, no loss — that is the discipline working.

4
00:00:13,740 --> 00:00:18,676
Why we grade in public: a forecast that is never checked is just content.

5
00:00:18,676 --> 00:00:24,085
Every morning call gets an evening verdict, and both go in the permanent record.

6
00:00:24,050 --> 00:00:28,260
On the day, the book is up 427 dollars.

7
00:00:28,260 --> 00:00:33,226
Digi International (DGII) leads, +2.0 percent.

8
00:00:33,226 --> 00:00:39,380
First Trust NASDAQ Cybersecurity ETF (CIBR) lags at -2.2.

9
00:00:39,380 --> 00:00:45,209
The checklist is clear: nothing requires action today.

10
00:00:45,209 --> 00:00:47,836
Today's lesson is noise versus signal.

11
00:00:47,836 --> 00:00:51,500
Daily moves are weather; the weekly trend is climate.

12
00:00:51,500 --> 00:00:54,542
The system never exits on a scary afternoon.

13
00:00:54,542 --> 00:01:00,073
It acts only on weekly closes, because that is where the real information lives.

14
00:01:00,023 --> 00:01:08,419
And here is the book itself — the actual holdings, identical to this morning's emailed report.

15
00:01:08,419 --> 00:01:18,334
11 positions, led by Avnet, ticker AVT, NVIDIA, ticker NVDA, First Trust NASDAQ Cybersecurity ETF, ticker CIBR.

16
00:01:18,334 --> 00:01:30,482
Every line carries its share count, its entry, and its exit — because a position without a defined exit is not a position, it is a hope.

17
00:01:30,441 --> 00:01:35,091
Now the part that makes this system different: the loop.

18
00:01:35,091 --> 00:01:37,416
Every morning OROS proposes.

19
00:01:37,416 --> 00:01:47,630
Every evening it grades its own call, journals the verdict, and adjusts its own risk before it is allowed to propose again.

20
00:01:47,630 --> 00:01:51,450
Inside the book today: 8 positions up, 3 down.

21
00:01:51,450 --> 00:01:53,692
Best, AVT at +2.15 percent.

22
00:01:53,692 --> 00:01:55,436
Worst, CIBR at -2.17.

23
00:01:55,436 --> 00:02:00,252
Both go in the journal — wins and losses get the same ink.

24
00:02:00,252 --> 00:02:07,227
The honest scoreboard reads: expectancy -2.98% per trade over 1010 graded positions.

25
00:02:07,227 --> 00:02:14,036
Because that record is soft, the system has throttled itself to 50% position size.

26
00:02:14,036 --> 00:02:19,019
Nobody told it to — full size must be earned back with wins.

27
00:02:19,019 --> 00:02:30,810
What tonight's read hands to tomorrow's open: 0 names close the day in Stage 2; position size stays cut to 50% until the record earns it back.

28
00:02:30,766 --> 00:02:32,910
Over to the sports desk.

29
00:02:32,910 --> 00:02:41,753
Day 62 of the race: three books, the same one hundred thousand simulated dollars, since July sixth.

30
00:02:41,753 --> 00:02:46,398
In first place, the index fund, in gray, at 102,926.

31
00:02:46,398 --> 00:02:53,008
Then the live self-rebuilding book, in blue, 1.1 thousand back at 101,810.

32
00:02:53,008 --> 00:02:59,886
Then the frozen first portfolio, the green line, 5.2 thousand back at 97,705.

33
00:02:59,886 --> 00:03:02,387
The race has no finish line.

34
00:03:02,387 --> 00:03:06,496
Same books, same dollars, back at it tomorrow.

35
00:03:06,496 --> 00:03:11,141
Every line starts the same day with the same dollar.

36
00:03:11,141 --> 00:03:12,928
Nothing is restated.

37
00:03:12,928 --> 00:03:21,835
On screen, three equity lines grown from the same starting dollar: the frozen first book, the live book, and the index fund.

38
00:03:21,797 --> 00:03:29,132
All figures are simulated paper trading, graded and journaled by the OROS agents with no human intervention.

39
00:03:29,132 --> 00:03:31,373
Nothing here is financial advice.

40
00:03:31,373 --> 00:03:34,362
From the closing desk — see you at the open.
